      "status": "draft",
      "stem": "The magnetic susceptibility of a paramagnetic material is $\\chi$ at temperature $27^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$. At what temperature (in $^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$) will its magnetic susceptibility become $\\frac{\\chi}{2}$?",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "content": "$54^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$",
          "is_correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "content": "$327^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$",
          "is_correct": true
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "content": "$600^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$",
          "is_correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "content": "$127^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$",
          "is_correct": false
        }
      ],
      "solution": "By Curie's Law, $\\chi \\propto \\frac{1}{T}$ where $T$ is in Kelvin.\n$T_1 = 27 + 273 = 300\\ \\mathrm{K}$.\nSince $\\chi_2 = \\frac{\\chi_1}{2}$, we must have $T_2 = 2 T_1 = 600\\ \\mathrm{K}$.\nIn Celsius, $T_2 = 600 - 273 = 327^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$."
